Problem: the previous Windows directoryless-session fix canonicalized only the first containment comparison. A persisted workspace beyond 260 characters could pass that fallback and then fail final validation because the base and target still used different Windows namespaces. The symlink walk also depended on lexical starts_with, so an alias or namespace difference could skip the entire defense.

Objective: make every downstream workspace barrier accept the same real directory identity while preserving strict containment and fail-closed symlink handling for local, UNC, long-path and custom-output roots.

Decisions: extracted path security logic into a focused 91-line module; kept the cheap lexical relative-path fast path; canonicalize both existing paths for fallback and final containment; inspect each target ancestor before testing canonical base identity; tolerate only NotFound for not-yet-created components; return the existing generic user error for every filesystem failure. After independent review, final validation was changed to canonicalize base and target exactly once so the same target is used for containment and is_dir, avoiding a path-replacement race between two reads.

Tests: added a real Windows end-to-end workspace above the legacy 260-character limit and a real Unix symlink-alias scenario. The Windows reproduction failed before the fix after directory creation, then passed. Final Rust suite: 2975 tests, 2971 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored. Target module: 13 passed on Windows. cargo clippy --lib --features windows-tests -- -A dead-code -D warnings passed; the unsuppressed strict profile still reports only pre-existing inactive CEF dead-code warnings. cargo fmt --check and git diff --check passed. Graphify AST graph updated.
